      id: 'introduction',
      title: '1. Introduction: Orientation — Daniel 10 within the Structure of the Book',
      content:
        'Daniel 10 introduces the final prophetic section of the book, extending through chapters 11 and 12. Although chapter 10 contains a remarkable vision of its own, its principal function lies in preparing both Daniel and the reader for the extensive revelation that follows. The three chapters constitute a single prophetic unit. Chapter 10 records Daniel\'s preparation and his encounter with a heavenly messenger. Chapter 11 unfolds an extraordinarily detailed prophecy concerning successive kingdoms and rulers, while chapter 12 concludes the vision by directing attention toward the resurrection, final deliverance, and the consummation of God\'s purposes.\n\nUnlike the earlier visions of chapters 2, 7, and 8, which begin almost immediately with symbolic imagery or prophetic explanation, this final revelation opens by drawing attention to Daniel himself. Before God reveals future history, Scripture first depicts the spiritual condition of the prophet receiving it. The emphasis upon prayer, humility, and divine strengthening demonstrates that prophetic revelation is never merely an intellectual exercise. It emerges within the context of communion with God.\n\nThe vision occurred "in the third year of Cyrus king of Persia" (Dan. 10:1), placing it around 536 BC. Cyrus had already issued his decree permitting the Jewish exiles to return to Jerusalem (Ezra 1:1\u20134), and a remnant had begun rebuilding both the city and the temple. Daniel, however, remained in Babylon. Scripture offers no explicit explanation for his decision, though his advanced age likely prevented such a demanding journey. Having been deported as a youth around 605 BC, Daniel would now have been nearing ninety years old.\n\nThis therefore represents the final recorded vision of Daniel\'s life. For almost seven decades he had served under Babylonian and Persian rulers, witnessing the rise and fall of empires exactly as God had revealed beforehand. Yet even after receiving numerous prophetic disclosures, Daniel continued to seek greater understanding. His example illustrates that spiritual maturity never removes the desire to know God\'s purposes more fully.\n\nThe opening verse also characterizes the revelation itself. Daniel states that "the message was true" and concerned "a great conflict" (Dan. 10:1). The Hebrew expression conveys more than military warfare. It suggests an extended struggle of exceptional magnitude. As the succeeding chapters reveal, this conflict unfolds on multiple levels. It encompasses the political struggles between earthly kingdoms, particularly Persia and Greece, yet simultaneously exposes an unseen spiritual battle occurring behind the movements of history. Earthly events and heavenly realities are therefore presented as inseparably connected.\n\nThis introductory chapter consequently functions as the theological gateway to chapters 11 and 12. Before describing the rise of kings, military campaigns, and political revolutions, God first reveals that history itself unfolds beneath His sovereign direction and amid an invisible conflict between heavenly and rebellious spiritual powers. What appears to humanity as ordinary political development is shown to possess dimensions that extend beyond human observation.\n\nThe chapter therefore establishes several themes that govern the remainder of the prophecy. God\'s knowledge extends beyond every future event. His providence remains active despite apparent delays. Prayer participates in realities that believers cannot always perceive. Finally, history advances neither randomly nor independently but according to the purposes of the God who declares "the end from the beginning" (Isa. 46:10).',
