        'Having described the historical circumstances surrounding the revelation, Daniel now recounts the extraordinary encounter that became the prelude to the final prophecy of the book. The narrative shifts from the prophet\'s season of mourning to an overwhelming manifestation of heavenly glory. Before Daniel receives any explanation concerning future kingdoms or unfolding historical events, he first encounters the majesty of the One who governs history itself.\n\nThe transition is significant. Throughout Scripture, divine revelation is frequently preceded by an encounter that impresses upon the recipient the holiness and authority of God. Moses stood before the burning bush before receiving his commission (Exod. 3). Isaiah saw the Lord enthroned before being sent to Israel (Isa. 6). Ezekiel beheld the divine chariot before receiving his prophetic ministry (Ezek. 1\u20132). Likewise, John saw the glorified Christ before recording the Apocalypse (Rev. 1). Daniel\'s experience belongs within this same pattern. The revelation of future events is grounded in the revelation of God\'s supremacy.',
