            '"Now on the twenty-fourth day of the first month, as I was by the side of the great river, that is, the Tigris..."\n\nDaniel carefully identifies both the date and location of the vision. The twenty-fourth day of the first month places the event shortly after the conclusion of Passover and the Feast of Unleavened Bread. While others may have celebrated Israel\'s deliverance from Egypt, Daniel remained occupied with fasting and prayer, his attention fixed upon the unfinished restoration of God\'s people.\n\nThe setting beside the Tigris River is equally noteworthy. The Tigris, known in Hebrew as Hiddekel, was one of the principal rivers of Mesopotamia and is first mentioned in Genesis 2:14 as one of the four rivers flowing from Eden. Throughout biblical history, the region surrounding the Tigris became associated with some of humanity\'s greatest imperial powers, including Assyria and later Persia. Daniel therefore receives this revelation in the very heart of the Gentile world, a fitting location for a prophecy that will trace the succession of world empires.\n\nThe text does not explain why Daniel was beside the river. He may have been travelling on official business or seeking solitude for prayer. Whatever the reason, the location becomes the stage upon which heaven momentarily intersects with earth.',
