          content:
            'The identity of this glorious figure has generated considerable discussion among interpreters.\n\nOne view understands the figure to be a pre-incarnate appearance of Christ, often referred to as a Christophany. Several observations support this interpretation.\n\nMost striking is the close correspondence between Daniel\'s description and John\'s vision of the glorified Christ in Revelation 1:13\u201316. Both figures possess radiant faces, eyes like flames of fire, feet resembling burnished bronze, and voices of overwhelming power. The similarities are too extensive to dismiss as coincidence. Furthermore, Scripture occasionally records appearances of the Son of God before His incarnation, such as the Commander of the Lord\'s army in Joshua 5 and the mysterious fourth figure walking with Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego in the fiery furnace (Dan. 3:25).\n\nOthers argue that the figure is a highly exalted angel, perhaps Gabriel, who previously interpreted Daniel\'s visions in chapters 8 and 9. This interpretation gains support from the surrounding context. The messenger later explains that he was delayed by "the prince of Persia" for twenty-one days and required assistance from Michael (vv. 13, 21). Such language appears difficult to reconcile with the absolute sovereignty of the divine Son, who requires no assistance in accomplishing His purposes.\n\nSome commentators therefore distinguish between the glorious figure of verses 5\u20136 and the angel who begins speaking in verse 10, suggesting that Daniel first beholds a manifestation of Christ before being addressed by an accompanying angelic messenger. Others regard the entire passage as describing one exalted angelic being throughout.\n\nScripture itself does not explicitly settle the question. Consequently, dogmatism is unwarranted. Regardless of one\'s conclusion, the theological emphasis remains unchanged. Daniel encounters a being whose glory far exceeds anything found within the created order, and the encounter leaves him utterly overwhelmed.',
