            '"Then he said to me, \'Do not fear, Daniel, for from the first day that you set your heart to understand, and to humble yourself before your God, your words were heard; and I have come because of your words.\'"\n\nThis verse provides one of Scripture\'s clearest statements concerning God\'s response to prayer.\n\nFirst, the messenger identifies the attitude that characterized Daniel\'s prayer. Daniel had "set his heart to understand." His fasting was not motivated by curiosity or the pursuit of hidden knowledge for its own sake. He sincerely desired to comprehend God\'s purposes concerning His people.\n\nSecond, Daniel had humbled himself before God. His prayers arose from dependence rather than self-confidence. Throughout Scripture, humility consistently precedes divine illumination. Pride assumes it already possesses understanding, whereas humility recognizes its need for God\'s instruction.\n\nThe messenger then reveals a remarkable truth.\n\n"From the first day... your words were heard."\n\nThe answer to Daniel\'s prayer was not delayed because God failed to notice him. Nor was heaven reluctant to respond. Divine attention was immediate. The very day Daniel began praying, God heard his petition and dispatched the answer.\n\nThis statement fundamentally shapes the theology of prayer presented in the chapter. Delayed answers must never be interpreted as evidence of divine indifference. From Daniel\'s perspective, three weeks had passed without any indication that heaven had responded. In reality, God had acted from the very beginning.',
