      id: 'purpose-of-mission',
      title: '4. The Purpose of the Heavenly Mission (Daniel 10:14)',
      content:
        'Although verse 14 concludes the messenger\'s explanation concerning the delay, it also serves as the thematic introduction to everything recorded in chapters 11 and 12. The statement functions as a heading for the final vision of Daniel, defining both its subject and its scope before the prophetic details begin.\n\n"Now I have come to make you understand what will happen to your people in the latter days, for the vision refers to many days yet to come."\n\nThe messenger identifies his mission in straightforward terms: he has come to give Daniel understanding. Throughout the book, Daniel consistently distinguished himself by seeking wisdom rather than merely receiving visions. Earlier revelations had left him troubled because he did not fully comprehend their meaning (Dan. 8:27). In chapter 9, he sought understanding through diligent study of Jeremiah\'s prophecy before Gabriel was sent to explain the vision of the seventy weeks. Now, in his final recorded vision, heaven once again responds to Daniel\'s desire for understanding.\n\nThis emphasis carries an important theological implication. Biblical prophecy was never intended simply to amaze or satisfy human curiosity. Its purpose is to reveal God\'s purposes so that His people may exercise faith, remain steadfast, and recognise His sovereignty over history. Revelation always serves a redemptive purpose.\n\n"Your People"\n\nThe messenger specifically states that the revelation concerns "your people." This expression unmistakably refers to Israel, God\'s covenant nation. Daniel had spent decades praying for Jerusalem, mourning over the exile, and interceding on behalf of his nation. It is therefore fitting that the final revelation should address the future of the very people whose restoration had occupied his prayers.\n\nNevertheless, the prophecy extends well beyond the borders of Judah. Persia, Greece, the Seleucid and Ptolemaic kingdoms, Rome, and later powers all appear within the vision. These empires are not introduced because Scripture seeks to provide a complete political history of the ancient world. Rather, they appear because their actions directly affect God\'s covenant people and the unfolding plan of redemption.\n\nThis perspective distinguishes biblical historiography from secular history. Scripture records world events according to their relationship with God\'s saving purposes. Empires rise to prominence, dominate nations, and eventually disappear, yet the central concern remains God\'s faithfulness to His covenant and His preservation of His people.\n\n"The Latter Days"\n\nThe phrase "the latter days" deserves careful attention. In the Old Testament, this expression often denotes the future from the prophet\'s perspective, though the precise horizon varies according to context.\n\nIn Daniel 10, the expression embraces an extended period rather than a single event. Chapter 11 begins with rulers immediately succeeding the Persian Empire, proceeds through the rise of Alexander the Great, the division of his kingdom, and the prolonged conflicts between the Seleucid and Ptolemaic dynasties. As the prophecy develops, its horizon broadens until chapter 12 speaks of unprecedented tribulation, the resurrection of the dead, final judgment, and everlasting deliverance.\n\nThe "latter days," therefore, encompass the entire course of history leading to God\'s ultimate triumph. Daniel is not merely informed about events a few years beyond his lifetime. He is permitted to view the broad sweep of redemptive history from the Persian period until the establishment of God\'s everlasting kingdom.\n\n"Many Days Yet to Come"\n\nThe messenger further explains that the vision concerns "many days yet to come." The expression underscores the long-range character of the prophecy.\n\nUnlike earlier revelations that focused upon relatively immediate historical developments, this final vision stretches across centuries. Many of the events Daniel is about to hear would not occur until long after his death. The rise of Alexander, the fragmentation of the Greek Empire, the wars between the kings of the North and South, Roman supremacy, and the climactic events preceding the end all belonged to a future Daniel himself would never witness.\n\nThis observation reinforces an important feature of biblical prophecy. God reveals history in advance not because His servants will personally experience every predicted event, but because successive generations of believers may recognise His sovereign direction as history unfolds. Every fulfilled prophecy becomes further evidence that history proceeds according to divine purpose rather than human chance.\n\nThe Function of Verse 14\n\nVerse 14 therefore serves as the transition from preparation to revelation. Everything preceding it has explained why the messenger was delayed, how Daniel\'s prayer was answered, and why spiritual conflict accompanies human history. Everything following it consists of the prophecy itself.\n\nThe order is deliberate. Before Daniel learns what will happen upon earth, he first learns what has already been happening in heaven. Only after the invisible conflict has been disclosed does God unveil the visible course of history.\n\nThis sequence reminds the reader that prophecy should never be interpreted merely as political prediction. Behind every earthly kingdom stands the sovereign God who directs history toward its appointed conclusion.',
