            '"And he said, \'O man greatly beloved, fear not! Peace be to you; be strong, yes, be strong!\' So when he spoke to me I was strengthened, and said, \'Let my lord speak, for you have strengthened me.\'"\n\nThe messenger again addresses Daniel as "greatly beloved." This repeated affirmation frames the chapter. Daniel first hears it when he is commanded to stand (v. 11), and he hears it again immediately before receiving the prophecy.\n\nThe reassurance unfolds in three parts.\n\nFirst, "Fear not." Throughout Scripture, divine revelation is frequently accompanied by this command. God\'s presence naturally inspires awe, yet His purpose toward His people is not destruction but fellowship.\n\nSecond, "Peace be to you." The Hebrew concept of peace (shalom) encompasses far more than the absence of fear. It denotes wholeness, well-being, and restored communion with God. Before Daniel receives further revelation, heaven first grants him peace.\n\nThird, "Be strong, yes, be strong." The repetition conveys urgency and encouragement. Daniel is not expected to find strength within himself. The strength comes through the messenger\'s words and God\'s sustaining grace.\n\nThe result is immediate.\n\n"When he spoke to me I was strengthened."\n\nThe emphasis falls not upon Daniel\'s resilience but upon the effectiveness of God\'s word. The same voice that had earlier caused him to collapse now restores him completely. Divine speech both humbles and strengthens according to God\'s purpose.\n\nOnly after receiving this strength does Daniel finally declare:\n\n"Let my lord speak, for you have strengthened me."\n\nDaniel is now prepared to receive the longest and most detailed prophecy in the Old Testament. His readiness is not the result of personal endurance but of divine enablement. The chapter therefore concludes this preparatory section by demonstrating that those whom God calls, He also sustains. The revelation that follows rests entirely upon God\'s initiative, God\'s strength, and God\'s grace.',
          quote: 'O man greatly beloved, fear not! Peace be to you; be strong, yes, be strong!',
          quoteCitation: 'Daniel 10:19',
