          content:
            'The messenger concludes by highlighting Michael\'s unique role.\n\n"No one upholds me against these, except Michael your prince."\n\nMichael has already appeared in verse 13 as one of the chief princes who came to assist during the conflict with Persia. Here, however, another important title is added.\n\nMichael is "your prince."\n\nThe pronoun refers to Daniel and, by extension, to God\'s covenant people. Michael occupies a special relationship to Israel as their heavenly protector and defender.\n\nThis role becomes even clearer in Daniel 12:1, where Michael stands on behalf of God\'s people during the climactic time of trouble preceding final deliverance. Jude identifies him as the archangel contending with Satan (Jude 9), while Revelation depicts him leading the armies of heaven against the dragon (Rev. 12:7\u20139).\n\nMany conservative Christian interpreters, particularly within the historic Protestant and Seventh-day Adventist traditions, identify Michael as the pre-incarnate Christ acting in His role as Commander of heaven\'s hosts. Others understand Michael to be the highest created archangel. Regardless of one\'s conclusion, the text clearly presents Michael as heaven\'s supreme defender of God\'s people and the chief opponent of the forces arrayed against them.',
