      title: '7. Theological Significance of Daniel 10',
      content:
        'The closing verses of Daniel 10 provide a fitting conclusion to the chapter\'s central themes.\n\nFirst, they reaffirm that history unfolds simultaneously on two levels. Human beings witness kings, battles, diplomacy, and political change. Heaven perceives the spiritual realities accompanying those same events.\n\nSecond, they demonstrate that God\'s sovereignty remains absolute. Although evil powers resist His purposes, their opposition is temporary and limited. Neither Persia nor Greece, nor the spiritual powers associated with them, can overturn what God has determined.\n\nThird, they reveal that God\'s people are never abandoned. Daniel had spent three weeks believing that heaven was silent. He now learns that God had been acting from the very first day. Even amid unseen conflict, divine providence continued directing events toward their appointed end.\n\nFinally, these verses prepare the reader for chapter 11. The extraordinary historical detail that follows should not be read merely as an account of political developments. Every king, every battle, every alliance, and every empire operates within the larger framework of God\'s sovereign rule and the ongoing conflict between the kingdom of God and the powers of evil.',
